TASK
- Find approximately 12 real academic papers that help answer or frame this research question.
- Search broadly across arXiv, Semantic Scholar, Crossref, OpenAlex, Google Scholar, publisher pages, institutional repositories, author pages, and official paper pages as useful discovery aids.
- Prefer an arXiv record when the same paper has a verified arXiv version, especially for AI/CS papers, but do NOT exclude a relevant paper merely because it is not on arXiv.
- Preserve the full research context; do not reduce the task to isolated keywords.
{% if "CURRENT RESEARCH ANSWER — PRIMARY FOLLOW-UP CONTEXT" in profile.context %}
- This is a FOLLOW-UP literature round. Treat the CURRENT RESEARCH ANSWER in the context as the primary state to improve, not as a conclusion to confirm.
- Identify search targets from unsupported claims, weak evidence, unresolved contradictions, boundary conditions, missing mechanisms, limitations, open questions, and explicit next-research needs in that answer.
- Prefer papers that could materially change, qualify, challenge, strengthen, or extend the current answer.
- Do NOT spend most of the candidate set retrieving papers that merely repeat claims already well supported by the current answer and approved knowledge.
- Use the approved Knowledge Cards and existing RQ-specific paper interpretations as supporting evidence for gap detection and duplicate avoidance.
{% endif %}
- Prioritize directly relevant recent work, then include a small number of foundational or adjacent papers when they materially help interpret the topic.
- Verify that every paper exists. Never invent titles, authors, publication years, DOI/arXiv IDs, URLs, or findings.
- Return bibliographic/abstract access and full-text access separately.
- `abstract_url` is the best verified page for bibliographic metadata and an abstract. Prefer an arXiv /abs/ page when a matching arXiv version exists; otherwise use a reliable publisher/DOI page or a scholarly metadata page such as Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, or Crossref when it exposes the abstract.
- `abstract_or_summary` should use a verified abstract whenever one is openly available from any reliable scholarly source; do not require arXiv. Write the content in Korean. If no abstract can be verified, provide only a short Korean discovery summary and make clear that it is a summary, not a quoted abstract.
- `full_text_url` should point to a verified machine-readable full text. Prefer actual open HTML full text when available. If HTML full text is unavailable but a direct public PDF is available, it is acceptable for `full_text_url` to be the same URL as `pdf_url`.
- `pdf_url` is a verified direct public PDF URL when available. Do not invent or infer a PDF URL from a landing page.
- `landing_url` is a verified human-facing paper/repository/publisher page that is useful for manually locating or downloading the paper when no direct open full text can be verified. Leave it empty if unnecessary or unverified.
- Never invent URLs. If access cannot be verified, return an empty string for that URL field.
- `citation_count` is optional. Include it only when a count is readily verifiable from a recognizable scholarly source such as OpenAlex or Semantic Scholar. Put that provider name in `citation_source`. Do not estimate citation counts.
- Google Scholar may be used as a discovery aid, but do not fabricate a Google Scholar citation count or Scholar URL when it cannot be directly verified.
- `quick_take` should state in Korean what the paper appears to contribute.
- `why_relevant` should explain in Korean why the researcher should read it for this Intent.
- `caution` should note indirect relevance, verification limits, or important scope limitations in Korean.
- Prefer papers that together cover the major approaches, recent trends, and important disagreements or limitations relevant to the question.

